    <h1>Foundation</h1>
    <p class="doc-subtitle">TCP/MR — v0.1</p>

    <p>
      The operating layer of the foundation. Not ontology, not technical specification.
      This is the <strong>minimal contour</strong> that any first participant, investor,
      or grant reviewer must understand identically.
    </p>

    <h2>Operating definition</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>TCP/MR · ARQ MVP</strong> is a minimal collaboration protocol
      in which participants themselves produce questions, decisions, traces, and
      rules of interaction, while the system helps route the path from an unclear
      problem to a recorded result.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        An AI-guided protocol for adaptive collaboration. It helps small groups
        formulate problems, distribute attention, record decisions, and adapt
        their rules of working together as complexity grows.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Theoretical lineage</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>TCP/MR</strong> stands for <strong>Trace</strong> and
      <strong>Mutual Recognition</strong>. The name mirrors TCP/IP in rhythm
      and length, and sits in two established scientific traditions:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Stigmergy</strong> — the <em>trace</em> layer.
        Studied in insect biology and distributed coordination: action leaves
        a mark in shared environment; the mark coordinates the next action
        without central control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mutual Recognition</strong> — the <em>recognition</em> layer.
        Lives in the biology of cooperation (kin recognition, cleaner fish ↔ client,
        fig ↔ pollinator wasp) and in international law (mutual recognition
        agreements in trade). Cooperation requires the ability to identify
        a partner who will cooperate back.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This aligns with Axelrod’s second condition for stable cooperation:
      <em>recognition of past partners</em>. Without recognition, cooperation
      degrades to one-shot exchange. TCP/MR makes recognition operational
      and verifiable through Trace.
    </p>

    <p>
      Internally we refer to the same protocol by its working codename,
      <em>BS Protocol</em> (Blockchain State). External and academic surfaces
      use TCP/MR. The two names denote the same artifact.
    </p>

    <h2>The practical route</h2>

    <p>A single collaboration cycle is eight steps:</p>

    <pre><code>Problem → Question → Trace → Signal → Coordination → Action → Result → Rule Update</code></pre>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Who moves it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Problem</strong></td><td>An internal unclarity</td><td>Any human</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Question</strong></td><td>Unclarity becomes a formulated Q</td><td>ARQ assists</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Trace</strong></td><td>Q and its first moves are recorded</td><td>ARQ + author</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Signal</strong></td><td>The Trace becomes a signal to others</td><td>System routes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Coordination</strong></td><td>Several participants pick up</td><td>Cell (3–5 ppl)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Action</strong></td><td>Execution of what was decided</td><td>Cell</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Result</strong></td><td>A recorded change</td><td>Cell</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rule Update</strong></td><td>Procedural correction is entered</td><td>Cell via BS-fork</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The MVP must demonstrate <strong>at least one full cycle</strong> on a group of 3–5.</p>

    <h2>The three structural invariants</h2>

    <p>These rules are not subject to forking. Without them the protocol collapses.</p>

    <h3>1. Question ≠ task.</h3>
    <p>
      A Q is a formulated <em>tension</em>, not a request for work. Without this,
      Q degrades into a Jira ticket and the protocol becomes a task manager.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Spark must carry cost-of-error.</h3>
    <p>
      Any proposed direction of resolution must explicitly state
      <em>“what becomes visible if this hypothesis is wrong.”</em>
      Without this, Trace accumulates opinions instead of verifiable moves.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Initiator ≠ Assembler.</h3>
    <p>
      The person who formulated a Q cannot be the one who closes its Trace.
      A structural anti-self-validation invariant. It prevents the cycle from
      collapsing onto a single person regardless of cell size.
    </p>

    <p>
      Everything else — windows, formats, roles, deadlines, storage layout —
      is determined by the cell itself and evolves through the
      <strong>Rule Update</strong> step.
    </p>

    <h2>Self-amending principle</h2>

    <p>
      A cell applies Q/S/T to its own procedure of collaboration. When something
      breaks or works poorly, that becomes a Q, passes through the cycle, and
      Rule Update changes the procedure for subsequent cycles.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>What evolves:</strong> collection windows, Trace formats, role composition, routing methods, closure criteria.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>What does not evolve:</strong> the three invariants above. They protect
      the very fact that evolution remains protocol-governed and does not collapse
      into arbitrariness.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 0 — what must be proven</h2>

    <p>Not <em>“that the protocol works at scale.”</em> Only three things:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Single-user route.</strong> One person passes through ARQ from fog to a precise Q and a closed Trace.</li>
      <li><strong>Small pack flow.</strong> A group of 3–5 completes a full cycle on a real task.</li>
      <li><strong>Live Rule Update.</strong> A cell detects a procedural gap, runs Q/S/T on the procedure itself, updates its log.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      When these three demonstrations exist, we can speak with Innosuisse,
      first participants, and serious audiences not as utopia but as mechanism.
    </p>

